.TH QUADRF-USB 8 "August 2026" "QuadRF" "QuadRF"
.SH NAME
quadrf-usb \- USB-gadget island on usb0
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B quadrf-usb
.RI [ start | stop | watch ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Follows the USB gadget link on usb0.
.B g_ether
creates the interface; a host is present when sysfs carrier is up or the
UDC reports
.BR configured .
On link the appliance takes
.B 10.55.0.1/24
and dnsmasq hands
.B 10.55.0.2
through
.B 10.55.0.10
to the attached machine. There is no default route, so a USB session
cannot take over outbound traffic. The published names match Ethernet
and Wi-Fi:
.BR quadrf.local ,
.BR quadrfd.local ,
.B quadrf-desktop.local
and
.BR QUADRF_TLS_DOMAIN .
.PP
usb0 is only ever an island. A gadget host is not a router, so there
is no DHCP-client path the way
.BR quadrf-ethernet (8)
has on eth0.
.PP
When the host unplugs, the address and the dnsmasq snippet are dropped
so avahi does not keep advertising
.B 10.55.0.1
on a dead link.
.PP
.B quadrf-usb.service
runs
.B watch
and re-evaluates when the gadget appears, enumerates or disconnects.
.B start
configures once, or signals the watcher if it is already running. The
ifupdown stanza in
.I /etc/network/interfaces.d/quadrf-usb0.cfg
calls
.B start
when usb0 appears.
.PP
Power the Pi from the UPS or pack. The Pi 5 USB-C jack is the gadget
data port and the power inlet; a laptop port must not supply the unit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/dnsmasq.d/quadrf-usb0-direct.conf
DHCP range and names, only while a host is enumerated.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR quadrf-ethernet (8),
.BR quadrf-hotspot (8),
.BR quadrf (1)
